$DIR/main.p:24:2: cannot pass &arr to g, which keeps it after returning
    &arr is a pointer to arr. arr is a local array of f (declared at main.p:23).
    g passes p to fmt.Println (main.p:19), which passes a pointer loaded from
    args[i] to Fprintln (print_linux.p:17), which passes a pointer loaded from
    args[i] to (*printer).doPrint (fmt.p:948), which passes a pointer loaded
    from args[i] to (*printer).printArg (fmt.p:915), which passes arg to a
    formatting method of what it walks (which may call main.(*Inner).String)
    (fmt.p:671), which stores i in package variable keep (main.p:14), so &arr
    would outlive arr.
    Go would move this memory to the heap, and its garbage collector would free
    it later. Plutonium has no garbage collector and never moves memory to the
    heap behind the program's back, so that every allocation is visible in the
    code.
    If g should keep its own copy of arr, change it to take the value, not a
    pointer, or an owner it can keep: an owned[[2]Inner] from new, a
    shared[[2]Inner], or a library owner such as a vec.Vec.
    (see "Views" in the Plutonium spec, section 18.3)
    (run "pluto explain E3003" for a longer explanation)
